# Pinned artifacts must survive a clone BYTE-FOR-BYTE.
#
# The curated design assets and the imported reference screens are pinned by SHA-256 in
# work/implementation/DESIGN-REFERENCE-MANIFEST.json and verified by tools/design-assets-check.mjs.
# The supplied design files are LF, and this repository is used on Windows with core.autocrlf=true,
# so without these rules git checks them out as CRLF: the digest would then differ from the reviewed
# artifact on every Windows clone, and the check would pass on one machine and fail on the next.
# `-text` disables end-of-line conversion in both directions, so the bytes are the artifact.
public/assets/design/**   -text
work/design-reference/**  -text

# Migration checksums record raw bytes. Keep future checkouts stable across platforms;
# provision.mjs separately recognises the exact LF/CRLF variants of older applied files.
server/migrations/*.sql text eol=lf

# MIRROR-B1PREP-01: the imported translation bundle is pinned by SHA-256 in
# content/library-translations/README.md and re-checked by the F2 importer, for the same reason as
# the design artifacts above: on Windows with core.autocrlf=true an unconverted checkout would
# change the digest. `-text` keeps the bytes identical to the reviewed artifact on every platform.
content/library-translations/*.json -text
# MEDIA-MOUNT-01 (A12(1)): the nine listening recordings are byte-exact artifacts whose sha256 the tracked
# package pins, and their PCM duration is verified at startup. Make the binary rule explicit rather than
# relying on git's heuristic, so no checkout can ever transform them.
*.wav binary
# PILOT-FEEDBACK-01 (FB-E): the vendored capture library is pinned by SHA-256 in
# public/assets/vendor/README.md and re-checked by tools/vendor-integrity-check.mjs, for the same reason as the
# artifacts above — on Windows with core.autocrlf=true an unconverted checkout would change the digest and the
# gate would fail on a platform rather than on a change. `-text` keeps the bytes identical on every platform.
# This was caught by git's own warning on the first commit of the file, which is exactly the class
# `tools/eol-hash-check.mjs` was written for.
public/assets/vendor/** -text
# Files a check hashes for a recorded digest must have the same bytes on every platform.
# Their recorded digests are the committed blob's (LF); without a rule a Windows working tree is
# CRLF and the hash differs, which made gates pass on one platform and fail on the other.
#
# EOL-HASH-AUDIT-01: `data/*.json` are the generator sources whose byte digest is recorded in
# `server/migrations/0011` (vocab), `0012` (nouns) and `0013`/`0014` (the seven guides), so every
# one of them has to check out identically everywhere. `data/seed.json` keeps its own line below
# for the two files that were pinned first; the rule above already covers it.
#
# ORDER MATTERS. In .gitattributes the LAST matching pattern wins, so a general rule appended after
# a deliberate pin silently overrides it. These patterns are disjoint from the `-text` pins above
# (`data/` vs `content/library-translations/`, `public/assets/design/`, `work/design-reference/`),
# and `git check-attr text eol -- <path>` is the evidence for that. If a future repo-wide `*.json`
# rule is ever wanted, it MUST go before the `content/library-translations/*.json -text` pin, or the
# translation bundle's recorded sha256 stops matching its bytes. `tools/eol-hash-check.mjs` fails
# by name when a path with a recorded digest is not pinned to a stable form.
data/seed.json          text eol=lf
data/*.json             text eol=lf
content/pool-01/*.json  text eol=lf
